// @run
// @log 1
// @hint Intentionally-unused variable `_u`
// WAS a frontier pin ("@emit-error ref valtype with no interned shape"); CLOSED by the
// generic-application nominal sidecar (typed-IR P1). Its old header read: "this program is
// REJECTED, and the DECLARED-alias spelling of the same union (`type U = Box<i32> | Tag`,
// pinned by `union-member-generic-application.vl`) compiles and runs."
//
// That header's diagnosis of the ASYMMETRY was right and its prescription was not. The
// asymmetry is real: a declared union's members live in a `UnionDecl`, where
// `canonEmitTypeNames` resolves a generic-application member to its instantiated shape; an
// INLINE union annotation is one `TypeRef` whose name is the pipe-joined member set,
// canonicalized by `canonEmitName`'s union arm — which is deliberately NOT given that
// rewrite, because the same arm canonicalizes `Box<i32> | null`, whose nullable-niche
// lowering resolves the application through its NAME.
//
// The prescription was to teach the NAME side to expand the application here too ("the
// member set split and each member resolved through the same rule the `UnionDecl` arm
// uses"). The fix came from the other direction entirely, and needed no new rule: the
// emitter's shape REGISTRATION walk is arena-driven (`tyToNominalName`), and the arena kept
// no record that an application had been written — so it registered the shape under the
// EXPANDED key `{v:i32}` while every classifier looked it up under `Box<i32>`, the name
// canon had left on the inline member. "No interned shape" was those two keys, not a
// missing capability. With the application banked nominally, the registration key IS
// `Box<i32>` and the two sides meet.
type Box<T> = { v: T }
type Tag = { t: i32 }

const _u: Box<i32> | Tag = { v: 1 }
print(1)
